MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The "if someone could get Stan running on Google sheets"
is the obstacle --- they only give you JavaScript to run on
their servers.  Emscripten is a way to transate C++ to JavaScript,
but it's not up to handling code of the complexity of Stan.

It would, on the other hand, be possible.  What we would need
for both this and cloudstan would be exactly the same:

  * the protobuf interface Allen and Krzysztof have been
    talking about to provide Stan-as-a-service over the wire

  * a way to host Stan on a server

    - this is expensive if we do it on something like
      the Amazon elastic compute cloud as anything other than
      a demo,

    - and it requires the appropriate scaling-out
      front-end so Stan could be elastic (add and subtract
      number of concurrent users)

  * client-side interface for either

    - server-side JavaScript for Google Pages

    - client-side JavaScript, CSS, and HTML for a web browser
      + some way to persist data (that's built into Google Pages
        and GitHub, for example)

Or if your buddies at Google can give us a backdoor
into their page API and host our C++, that'd make it a lot
easier. :-)

- Bob

