Ugh, all of this self congratulation on Twitter between Murray and the autograd/theano
with no discussion of Stan is still very disappointing; it’s taking everything I have to not
write a snarky response.  To be clear, Matt and David and the autograd team are all
great, just disappointed that all of this discussion is happening in a Stan-vacuum.  Not
sure what else we have to do to be acknowledged by those communities.

On Feb 25, 2016, at 10:41 PM, Dustin Tran <dustinv...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Not sure how helpful this is, but Matt Johnson implemented Iain Murray’s algorithm just today in autograd. (Or did he implement one of many algorithms? I didn’t look closely)
>
> https://github.com/HIPS/autograd/blob/master/autograd/numpy/linalg.py#L80
>
> Dustin
> > On Feb 25, 2016, at 5:25 PM, Robert Trangucci <robert.t...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > I think it would be great to look into the blocking algorithm proposed in Murray.
> >
> > As for the blog post, perhaps it'd be useful to let people know that inference on latent GPs has been sped up/slimmed down, and give a few examples of runtime differences.
> >
> > Maybe Michael's Seville BDA hierarchical GPs?
> >
> > Rob
> >
> > > On Feb 25, 2016, at 5:17 PM, Aki Vehtari <aki.v...@aalto.fi> wrote:
> > >
> > > Yes! Thanks Rob!
> > >
> > > And I did not want to imply that potential additional improvements should have top priority. I just wanted to point out this paper for reference if someone at some point has time and we want to get more speed (e.g. with block algorithms).
> > >
> > > Aki
> > >
> > > > On Friday, February 26, 2016 at 12:13:41 AM UTC+2, robert.trangucci wrote:
> > > > I think Aki was referring to the fact that we've made autodiffing through the Cholesky faster and less memory-intensive than previous versions of Stan (where we didn't have an explicit reverse-mode implementation).
> > > >
> > > > We've implemented Giles algorithm which he references in the paper.
> > > >
> > > > Rob
> > > >
> > > > > On Feb 25, 2016, at 5:07 PM, Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:
> > > > >
> > > > > I have no idea what we could add.  I was just reacting to the comments by Aki and Mike that Stan is faster and uses less memory than was was done in that paper.  If that’s really true, it coudl be worth our making that clear.
